{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"IAM Policy Simulator moves to the IAM console and adds additional capabilities - AWS","context":"AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) has updated IAM Policy Simulator, a tool used to test and validate IAM policies before deployment. The changes include moving the simulator to the IAM console, adding the ability to test service control policies (SCPs), and providing more flexibility for simulating scenarios. The simulator is now integrated into the IAM console, allowing users to test policies alongside identity and policy management. SCPs can now be included in simulations to test how they interact with identity and resource policies, and the API can be used to test how condition keys affect outcomes. New flexibility allows users to exclude specific policies for \"what if\" scenarios and receive per-policy decisions for identity and resource-based policies in cross-account simulations. These features help teams automate policy unit testing, detect over-permissive access, and validate guardrails with greater confidence. The updates are available in all regions where IAM Policy Simulator is offered. To learn more, consult the resources provided in the document","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/07/iam-policy-simulator-iam-console/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"aws-iam","skill_description":"Provides verified corrections for IAM behaviors that AI agents frequently get wrong — policy evaluation edge cases, trust policy gotchas, STS session limits, Organizations quirks, and SAML/MFA specifics. Also provides structured workflows for IAM role management and baseline policy generation from application source code or a Terraform plan JSON. Covers condition operator safety (ForAnyValue/ForAllValues with Null checks), bucket policy deny patterns (VPC endpoint restrictions, org paths), confused deputy protection, and service role creation for AWS services (Glue, CloudTrail, Lambda, ECS, etc.) with aws:SourceAccount/aws:SourceArn trust conditions. Applies when creating IAM roles, writing IAM or bucket policies, generating policies from application source code or a Terraform plan JSON, working with STS, Organizations, or condition operators, or any task needing a service or execution role. Does not cover non-IAM authorization like Cognito user-pool policies or app-level RBAC.\n\n\nServices: iam, sts, organizations\nTasks: deploy, configure, secure, policy-generation, permissions, source-code-to-policy, terraform-plan-to-policy\nPersona: developer, devops, architect, security-engineer\nWorkload: serverless, containers, data-analytics, security\n*","skill_name":"aws-iam"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"What's New at AWS - Cloud Innovation & News","context":"AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) has introduced three new global condition keys to simplify network perimeter control. These keys - aws:VpceAccount, aws:VpceOrgPaths, and aws:VpceOrgID - enable users to ensure that requests to AWS resources or from identities are made through VPC endpoints. The condition keys offer varying levels of granularity, allowing users to implement network perimeter controls at an account, organization path, and entire organization level. These controls automatically scale with VPC usage, eliminating the need to update policies as VPC endpoints are added or removed. The condition keys can be used with various types of policies, including service control policies (SCPs), resource control policies (RCPs), resource-based policies, and identity-based policies. They are supported for a select set of AWS services and are available in all commercial AWS Regions where those services support AWS PrivateLink. For more information on these new condition keys and supported services, users are encouraged to visit the AWS IAM documentation and AWS blog","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/08/aws-iam-new-vpc-endpoint-condition-keys/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"Amazon OpenSearch UI now supports Network Access Control - AWS","context":"Amazon OpenSearch Service now supports network access controls for OpenSearch UI applications. OpenSearch UI is a managed web service for search, analytics, and observability across multiple AWS data sources. Network access controls enable restricting access to OpenSearch UI applications to approved networks using IAM condition keys (aws:SourceVpce, aws:SourceVpc, and aws:SourceIp). Access can be enforced at three levels: identity-based policies, VPC endpoint policies, and resource control policies (RCPs). RCPs can block off-network users before authentication, preventing unauthorized access. Network access controls are available in all AWS Regions where OpenSearch UI is available. For more information, refer to the \"Restricting network access to OpenSearch UI applications\" section in the Amazon OpenSearch Service Developer Guide","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/08/opensearch-ui-network-access-control/"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"AWS introduces additional policy details to access denied error messages - AWS","context":"AWS has updated access denied error messages in scenarios where the resources are within the same account and organization. The new error messages include the Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of the specific IAM and AWS Organizations policy responsible for the denied access. This enhancement allows for faster identification and resolution of access denied issues, particularly in cases where multiple policies of the same type are involved. The error message now provides the ARN for various policy types, including Service Control Policies (SCP), Resource Control Policies (RCP), identity-based policies, session policies, and permission boundaries. This new feature will be rolled out across all AWS services and regions. For more information, refer to the IAM documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/01/additional-policy-details-access-denied-error/"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Checking AWS docs on IAM policy simulator evaluation limitations."}
